Open the `core.sqlite_dos` database in WAL mode, with a usable busy timeout.

A profile's database is written by the daemon workers and read and written by
every `verdi` invocation at the same time.  `create_sqla_engine` passes no
`connect_args` and sets no journal pragma, so `core.sqlite_dos` runs on the two
SQLite defaults, and neither one supports that:

* The default journal mode is `DELETE`, a rollback journal.  A reader holding
  `SHARED` prevents a writer from taking `EXCLUSIVE`, so an open read
  transaction anywhere blocks every write.
* The default busy timeout is the DB-API's five seconds.  That is a normal
  duration for a daemon worker's transaction, so the wait ends in an error
  rather than in the lock.

The result is a write that fails for no reason the user can act on:

    sqlalchemy.exc.OperationalError: (sqlite3.OperationalError) database is locked
    [SQL: UPDATE db_dbauthinfo SET auth_params=? WHERE db_dbauthinfo.id = ?]

That statement is `Computer.configure()`, which is about as small a write as
this storage plugin ever makes.  It failed because a daemon worker happened to
be running a calculation against the same file.

The damage does not stop at the failing statement.  The storage backend's
session is shared, and a failed flush leaves it `DEACTIVE`, so everything after
it on the same connection reports

    sqlalchemy.exc.PendingRollbackError: This Session's transaction has been
    rolled back due to a previous exception during flush.

and names the original lock rather than itself.  One contended write therefore
reads as two unrelated defects, in two unrelated places.

None of this needs AiiDA to reproduce.  Two connections to one file, the first
holding an open read transaction, the second issuing an `UPDATE`: under the
default journal the write raises `database is locked` as soon as the busy
timeout expires, and under `journal_mode=WAL` it commits in milliseconds.

WAL is the mode for exactly this access pattern -- concurrent readers and one
writer -- and a longer busy timeout turns writer-against-writer contention from
an error back into a wait.  Both are set from the `connect` event, beside the
`case_sensitive_like` pragma that `create_sqla_engine` already sets there, and
both call sites in this module go through one constructor.  That last part is
not tidiness: the two pragmas have different lifetimes.  `journal_mode` is
recorded in the database header, so whichever engine opens the file first
decides it for everyone, while `busy_timeout` is per connection and has to be
set on every one.  A wrapper is what keeps the migrator and the session factory
from disagreeing about either.

`_backup_storage` needed the accompanying change.  It rsyncs `filepath_database`
alone, and under WAL a committed transaction can still be in the `-wal` sidecar
rather than in that file, which would make the backup silently short of the most
recent commits rather than fail.  Checkpointing with `TRUNCATE` folds the log
back into the database and empties it, so the one file the rsync takes is again
complete.  `TRUNCATE` waits for readers and reports rather than raises when it
gives up, so the case where it cannot finish is logged instead of ignored.

Two things WAL does not break.  A database on a filesystem with no shared
memory -- a network mount -- keeps the journal mode it had: SQLite reports the
mode in the returned row and does not raise, so such a profile goes on working
as before.  And `journal_mode` persisting in the header is what makes an older
AiiDA opening the same profile behave consistently, rather than a hazard.

