+        # Only ``filepath_database`` is copied below, and in WAL mode a committed
+        # transaction may still live in the ``-wal`` sidecar rather than in that
+        # file.  Checkpointing folds the log back into the database, so the single
+        # file the rsync takes is complete.
+        #
+        # ``TRUNCATE`` waits for readers -- for up to ``BUSY_TIMEOUT_MILLISECONDS``
+        # -- and then reports rather than raises, so the case where it gives up
+        # has to be caught here or the backup is silently short of the most recent
+        # commits.  The two counts say how short: a reader pinning an old snapshot
+        # holds the checkpoint at that snapshot's frame, leaving everything after
+        # it in the log alone.  They are reported either way, because a busy
+        # checkpoint that still copied every frame leaves a complete database file
+        # and only an untruncated log, and the operator cannot tell those apart
+        # from the warning alone.
+        LOGGER.report('Checkpointing the write-ahead log')
+        busy, log_pages, checkpointed = self.get_session().execute(text('PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE);')).one()
+        if busy:
